"Stand on the coast of Changhua on a winter day, and the northeast monsoon pushes so hard that you can lean into it. Far out at sea, beyond the horizon, more than a hundred giant turbines turn in that same wind. In June 2026, Taiwan finished building its 500th offshore wind turbine. How does moving air become the electricity in your home? People have used the wind for thousands of years to sail boats, and for more than a thousand years to grind grain with windmills. A modern wind turbine does a new job: it spins a generator, just like the ones in Lesson 2. And where does wind come from? The sun heats the land, the sea, and the air unevenly. Warm air rises, cooler air rushes in to take its place, and that moving air is wind. In a way, wind power is solar power in disguise. Turbine blades are not flat paddles. Each one is shaped like an airplane wing. As air flows over the curved blade, it creates a force called lift, which pulls the blade sideways. Lift turns the rotor around its hub. In the turbines off Changhua, the rotor turns a ring of strong magnets past coils of wire, which is the generator from Lesson 2 in a giant size. There is no gearbox at all. These machines are enormous. Each blade on the Greater Changhua turbines is about 81 meters long, and the rotor sweeps an area about the size of three soccer fields. The rotor turns slowly, only about ten times a minute at most, but the tips of the blades travel at more than 300 kilometers an hour, faster than Taiwan's high-speed rail. The power in the wind grows very fast with its speed. If the wind blows twice as fast, it carries eight times as much power, because the power grows with the speed multiplied by itself three times. A turbine like these starts making electricity at about 3 meters per second and reaches full power at about 12 to 13. In stronger winds, it turns its blades to spill some of the wind, and in a typhoon it turns them edge-on and stops to protect itself. No turbine can catch all of the wind's energy. The limit is about 59%, because the air has to keep moving past. Taiwan has some of the best offshore wind in the world. One study found that 16 of the world's 20 windiest offshore sites are in the Taiwan Strait. By June 2026, Taiwan had built 500 offshore turbines, and in 2025 they made more than 10 billion kilowatt-hours of electricity for the first time. The wind does not always blow, and building in the sea is hard and expensive. But whenever the monsoon rushes across the strait, those giant wings turn it into power.": "60ee35a738db",
"Taiwan has some of the best offshore wind in the world. One study found that 16 of the world's 20 windiest offshore sites are in the Taiwan Strait. By June 2026, Taiwan had built 500 offshore turbines, and in 2025 they made more than 10 billion kilowatt-hours of electricity for the first time. The wind does not always blow, and building in the sea is hard and expensive. But whenever the monsoon rushes across the strait, those giant wings turn it into power.": "988a0d1993a7",
